course: dictionary
lesson: 1487
title: Cause and effect
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A cause is what makes something happen. The thing that happens because of it is the effect. In «The cause of the fire was a candle», the candle is the cause, and the fire is the effect it made happen.
explain: Cause can also be a verb, a doing word. «Ice can cause accidents» means ice can make accidents happen. To find a cause, ask yourself: what made this happen?
explain: Cause has a third meaning: a goal or idea that people support, as in «a good cause». Here it does not make anything happen. It means something people believe in and work for.
fact: A cause is what makes something happen, and the result is called the effect.
fact: «Cause» can be a noun, as in «the cause of the fire», or a verb, as in «ice can cause accidents».
fact: «Cause» can also mean a goal people support, as in «for a good cause».
fact: In a fair test you change only one thing, so if the result changes, you know what caused it.
fact: Useful phrases: cause and effect, the cause of, cause trouble.
exercise: In «The cause of the fire was a candle», what is the cause? | The night | The smoke | * The candle | The fire
exercise: «Ice can cause accidents.» What does cause mean here? | To paint something | To stop something from moving | To hide something | * To make something happen
exercise: In «a good cause», what does cause mean? | To break something | A thing that makes a fire start | A test that is unfair | * Something people believe in and work for
test: In a fair test, why do you change only one thing? | * So that if the result changes, you know what caused it | So you can ignore the results | To make the test longer | To use more equipment
test: When something doesn't work, what should you check first? | The weather forecast for next year | Your favourite song | The colour of the walls | * The simple causes, such as whether the socket is switched on
exam: In «The hairs of some caterpillars cause rashes», what is the effect? | Some people | * Rashes | Caterpillars | The hairs
